#: library/processes/sample-import.bpmn:41
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "One decision, and it is the one no file answers: what the sample is FOR. Which items, into which store, and whether the result is meant to last. The last question decides the destination, and asking it after the copy exists would mean moving a copy that was already landed somewhere under the wrong obligations."
msgstr ""

#: library/processes/sample-import.bpmn:180
#. Diagram label
msgid "permanent"
msgstr ""

#: library/processes/sample-import.bpmn:87
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "Read off materialize-remote's end state, never re-decided. `referenced` (any gate refused or unknown) ends the sample here; `materialized` continues."
msgstr ""

#: library/processes/sample-import.bpmn:133
#. Diagram label
msgid "Record what failed"
msgstr ""

#: library/processes/sample-import.bpmn:163
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "Refresh is not re-import: what changed upstream, what changed locally since, and what to do when both did. The shared subprocess answers all three, and re-asks the five gates, since a licence can change and a collection can grow past the size agreed."
msgstr ""

#: library/processes/sample-import.bpmn:161
#. Diagram label
msgid "Refresh materialized content"
msgstr ""

#: library/processes/sample-import.bpmn:172
#. Diagram label
msgid "Refreshed"
msgstr ""

#: library/processes/sample-import.bpmn:28
#. Diagram label
msgid "Sample import into a structured data store"
msgstr ""

#: library/processes/sample-import.bpmn:152
#. Diagram label
msgid "Sample imported and tested"
msgstr ""

#: library/processes/sample-import.bpmn:68
#. Diagram label
msgid "Scope the sample: items, store, permanence"
msgstr ""

#: library/processes/sample-import.bpmn:94
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "The answer from Task_Scope. There is no default, and none is needed here: Task_Scope does not complete until permanence is stated, so an unscoped sample never reaches this gateway."
msgstr ""

#: library/processes/sample-import.bpmn:65
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "The caller names a source (ideally one with a source descriptor in the declared sources/ of the layer that owns the corpus) and a reason to try it. Nothing about size, licence or permanence is assumed at this point."
msgstr ""

#: library/processes/sample-import.bpmn:118
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "The part only this process does. Load the landed sample into the chosen store and check what an import can get wrong: every item arrived (count against the request), identifiers survived (the descriptor's authoritative ones), structure validates against the store's schema, and provenance (source, revision, fixity) is attached to each node. A check that could not run is a failure, not a pass."
msgstr ""

#: library/processes/sample-import.bpmn:31
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "The SDLC for testing an import of a SAMPLE of a remote source into a knowledge graph or other structured store. It covers data size, retention, source loss and copyright. General, not WHO-specific: IRIS is the worked instance, and who-iris/ is what this is exercised on. THE GATES ARE CALLED, NOT COPIED. Size, restrictions, copyright, retention and source loss live once, in materialize-remote.bpmn. This process branches on that call's OUTCOME, so a gate refused there is a refusal here and there is no second copy to drift. WHERE A SAMPLE LANDS depends on whether it is meant to last. A permanent sample goes to library/ and is kept current by refresh-materialized.bpmn. A trial goes to fsh-guts/: kept, addressable, never published, and never refreshed, because a trial is evidence of what the source looked like when it was tried."
msgstr ""

#: library/processes/sample-import.bpmn:158
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "The second entry. Only library/ copies arrive here; fsh-guts/ trials are never refreshed."
msgstr ""

#: library/processes/sample-import.bpmn:78
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "The shared subprocess. It resolves the request against the source descriptor, then asks size, restrictions, copyright, retention and source loss, and either fetches with fixity or leaves the content referenced. This process does not restate any of that; it waits for the outcome."
msgstr ""

#: library/processes/sample-import.bpmn:69
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "Three answers, recorded before anything is fetched. WHICH ITEMS: named by the source's identifiers, or by a subset strategy its descriptor supports. WHICH STORE: the knowledge graph or other structured store the import is tested against. PERMANENT OR TRIAL: a permanent sample lands in library/ and is refreshed; a trial lands in fsh-guts/ and is not. The purpose materialize-remote asks for (working or archival) is asked there, not here."
msgstr ""

#: library/processes/sample-import.bpmn:181
#. Diagram label
msgid "trial"
msgstr ""

#: library/processes/sample-import.bpmn:134
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "Which check failed, on which items, and the evidence. The landed copy is not removed: a failed import is information about the source or the store, and deleting the copy would destroy the evidence."
msgstr ""

#: library/processes/sample-import.bpmn:178
#. Diagram label
msgid "yes"
msgstr ""
